    "common_form_notes": "The dominant architectural/structural extrusion alloy — T-slot rail (e.g. 8020-style), tube, and custom extruded profiles. Weaker than 6061-T6 but extrudes into much finer detail and has substantially higher thermal conductivity; not typically stocked as sheet/plate the way 6061 is.",
    "usage_frequency": "Common",
    "notes": "T5 temper: cooled from an elevated-temperature shaping process (extrusion) then artificially aged, without a separate solution heat-treatment step. Melting point is solidus (616 °C); liquidus 654 °C. Shear modulus (25.8 GPa per source) is not an independently stored value; computing G = E/(2(1+ν)) from the reported Young's modulus and Poisson's ratio gives ≈25.9 GPa, consistent with the source. Electrical conductivity derived from source resistivity (3.16×10⁻⁶ Ω·cm) via %IACS (International Annealed Copper Standard) = (1.7241 µΩ·cm / 3.16 µΩ·cm) × 100 ≈ 54.6% IACS."
  },
  "typical_usage": "T-slot extrusion framing for robotics/mechatronics chassis and enclosures, architectural trim and window/door framing, tubing, heat sinks (high thermal conductivity), and other extruded structural shapes where moderate strength and excellent extrudability/surface finish matter more than peak strength.",
